{
 "section": "10",
 "slug": "10-02-derinkuyu",
 "heading": "## 10 — #2 DERINKUYU, TÜRKIYE",
 "text": "Number two: Derinkuyu, in Cappadocia. Beneath the town lies an underground complex of multiple levels. The parts open today go down more than fifty metres, and the full complex is estimated to reach around eighty-five. Rooms, stables, storage areas, wells, and ventilation shafts carrying air to the deepest levels. Huge round stone doors could be rolled shut from inside. The rock is soft volcanic tuff, carved by hand. The complex grew over centuries and was greatly expanded in the Byzantine era as shelter in dangerous times. You'll often hear it held twenty thousand people. That number isn't securely established, and it isn't a proven population living here at once. Derinkuyu was carved out a basket at a time. Number one went the other way: single stones of almost unbelievable size.",
 "text_chars": 792,
 "previous_text": "So far, everything went up. Number two goes down.",
 "next_text": "Number one: Baalbek, in Lebanon's Beqaa Valley. Beneath the Roman Temple of Jupiter lies a stone platform.",
